DDEESSCCRRIIPPTTIIOONN
     nnmm prints the symbol table of each _f_i_l_e. Each _f_i_l_e may be a
     linked ll.oouutt object or executable, or an archive (a file
     beginning with the archive magic number) made by aarr; for an
     archive, nnmm prints the symbols of every member except
     __.SSYYMMDDEEFF. With no _f_i_l_e argument, nnmm reads ll.oouutt.

     By default, nnmm prints only symbols whose names look like C
     identifiers (a trailing underscore is stripped before prin-
     ting, or the name is at least seven characters long); the -aa
     option disables this filtering.  Symbols are sorted by name
     unless -nn or -pp is given.  Each line gives the symbol's seg-
     ment (one of ssii,  ppii,  bbii,  for defined symbols, blank for a
     common symbol, or uu or UU for an undefined reference,
     capitalized if the symbol is global) and either its address
     or, for a common symbol, its size, followed by the symbol
     name.

     When more than one _f_i_l_e is given, or a _f_i_l_e is an archive,
     nnmm prints a heading line naming the file or archive member
     before its symbols, unless -oo was given, in which case the
     file or member name is instead prepended to every symbol
     line.

     -aa   Print all symbols, including ones that are not C iden-
          tifiers.

     -dd   Print only defined symbols (suppress undefined referen-
          ces).

     -gg   Print only global (external) symbols.

     -nn   Sort symbols numerically by address, instead of by
          name.

     -oo   Prepend the file (or, for an archive member, the mem-
          ber) name to each output line instead of printing it as
          a heading.

     -pp   Print symbols in the order they occur in the symbol
          table; do not sort them.

     -rr   Reverse the sort order.

     -uu   Print only undefined symbols.

